## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked
This tests precise DP construction for selecting k disjoint subarrays with per-subarray penalty, a common optimization variant.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint subarray DP with two states
- Transition derivation for starting/continuing a subarray
- Space/time trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], t>0 ? best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] : NEG);
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t] - c);
    }
  }
  return {value: best[n][k], subs: null};
}
```

